1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:19,320
The saved Week 15 plan asks you to connect incident systems, rapid
triage, radio communication, changing patient priorities and an
ethical debrief. The goal is not to memorize colors in isolation; it
is to keep a shared plan functioning as the patient count and
available resources change.

2
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:42,552
The opened AET nine A lesson actually teaches Transport Logistics and
Flight Physiology; that content has its own course lesson. Moodle
labels the link as MCI, so the label and opened body differ. Here, the
six-section MCI Response module is a topic-matched support source, not
a claim that an additional nine A MCI body was recovered.

3
00:00:42,552 --> 00:00:59,472
A mass casualty incident is not defined only by a large number. BCEHS
describes an MCI as a situation in which the initial response becomes
overwhelmed; the relevant question is whether the resources on hand
can manage the number and needs of patients.

4
00:00:59,472 --> 00:01:17,136
A small rural collision may exceed one crew’s capacity, while a larger
event in a well-resourced area may initially be manageable. The answer
can change as more ambulances arrive, patients are found, hazards
emerge or hospitals report reduced capacity.

5
00:01:17,136 --> 00:01:34,200
Think of the incident as several linked flows: information moves to
command, patients move toward safe extraction and care, and units move
only after receiving an assignment. A blockage in any one flow can
create a queue or leave a patient untracked.

6
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:46,080
Your task is to keep those flows visible. Do not improve one local
measure, such as rapid tagging, by sending people into an unsafe area
or creating an unreported transport route.

7
00:01:46,080 --> 00:02:03,456
The school index retains the A05 assignment. BCEHS now redirects A05
mass-casualty material to its Major Incident Management section, so
the current role information and posted START/JumpSTART job aids are
the operational reference used here.

8
00:02:03,456 --> 00:02:22,632
The AET course introduces useful concepts, but some older generic
triage descriptions differ from the current local card. For practice,
follow the exact BC tool and its 30-second target. Do not combine
thresholds or actions from multiple systems because they look
familiar.

9
00:02:22,632 --> 00:02:40,392
The current BCEHS MCI page names an Incident Commander and a Triage
Leader. The commander maintains the overall link with the
communication centre; the triage leader uses the approved tool and
reports the casualty picture while coordinating movement toward the
ambulance extraction point.

10
00:02:40,392 --> 00:02:57,072
A later ambulance checks in before receiving an assignment. Depending
on the incident, command may assign treatment, transport, scribe or
another function. These are tasks in a response plan, not roles to
claim because you arrived first or prefer them.

11
00:02:57,072 --> 00:03:25,344
METHANE begins with whether a major incident has been declared: yes or
no. If not, the BCEHS aid calls for an ETHANE report. The remaining
fields are exact location, type of incident, hazards, access and
egress, number and condition of casualties, and emergency services
present or required. State declaration status truthfully within your
assigned role, and identify estimates and unknowns.

12
00:03:25,344 --> 00:03:40,368
A report is not a polished narrative. State what is known, what is
unknown and what would change the plan. Update after a hazard shift,
new patient group, access problem or material change in available
transport.

13
00:03:40,368 --> 00:03:53,184
The purpose of check-in is to prevent a second crew from entering,
parking or treating independently of the common plan. Confirm where to
stage, whom to report to and which task you own before starting.

14
00:03:53,184 --> 00:04:09,888
When command or a function changes hands, summarize current hazards,
patient count, work in progress, resource limits and unresolved
decisions. The incoming person confirms the transfer; the team is then
told which reporting line applies.

15
00:04:09,888 --> 00:04:24,696
The triage leader’s current BCEHS function includes prioritizing
patients and coordinating movement from the incident area to the
ambulance extraction point. The triage pass is brief; treatment and
transport functions follow the incident plan.

16
00:04:24,696 --> 00:04:40,416
Do not turn the first pass into a long head-to-toe exam, an unapproved
intervention or a transport destination decision made alone. A
category communicates priority; it does not replace a secondary
assessment by the appropriate role.

17
00:04:40,416 --> 00:04:57,384
For an adult who can walk on command and has no major injury
identified in that brief sort, use the Minor branch and follow the
plan for that group. Walking is a rapid branch, not a guarantee of
health or permission to assign the patient an unsupervised task.

18
00:04:57,384 --> 00:05:21,480
For a non-walker, check breathing. If absent, position the airway as
the current card specifies: persistent apnea is tagged Deceased;
breathing that begins is Immediate. If breathing is present, follow
the posted sequence: rate, radial pulse, then simple-command response.
Do not add capillary refill or other thresholds to this card.

19
00:05:21,480 --> 00:05:38,832
The test is whether you can name the first decisive branch, not
whether you can collect every possible observation. Once a patient
meets an Immediate criterion, mark and move on according to the role
and plan; do not spend the remaining 30 seconds proving the same
category again.

20
00:05:38,832 --> 00:05:54,024
If the observed information does not fit the card or you cannot safely
interpret it, say so, follow the local escalation process and obtain
the next authorized decision. Do not silently substitute an algorithm
you remember from another course.

21
00:05:54,024 --> 00:06:19,320
The school plan explicitly names JumpSTART for the bus-crash scenario.
Use the current pediatric card. When spontaneous breathing is absent,
position the airway first. If breathing resumes, assign Immediate. If
apnea persists, check for a pulse. The five rescue breaths apply only
to that persistent-apnea branch with a palpable pulse, followed by a
respiratory recheck.

22
00:06:19,320 --> 00:06:36,264
The current chart uses respiratory-rate bounds of 15 to 45 per minute
and AVPU-only neurologic sorting in the relevant branch. Follow the
graphic in order; do not use adult rate thresholds, a full GCS or a
remembered age cut-off as a substitute.

23
00:06:36,264 --> 00:06:51,792
The five-breath step is a specific JumpSTART branch, not a general
permission to delay response for all children. It is not an adult
START step. The local chart, training, assigned role and available
equipment govern how the branch is performed.

24
00:06:51,792 --> 00:07:09,600
If a child cannot walk because of developmental stage or disability,
do not mistake inability to walk for a triage category by itself.
Follow the tool and incident plan, including appropriate secondary
review for a child who cannot communicate or manage basic needs.

25
00:07:09,600 --> 00:07:22,800
A rapid category is not a definitive diagnosis, prognosis or promise
of transport order. It communicates a priority under a particular
patient picture, tool and resource situation.

26
00:07:22,800 --> 00:07:39,168
Different systems use different words, colors or criteria. The current
BCEHS charts use the labels shown on those charts. Do not translate a
local Deceased label into an unverified “expectant” policy or infer
that a patient should be abandoned.

27
00:07:39,168 --> 00:07:57,360
The AET learning body discusses MARCH and lists examples of airway,
breathing, circulation and warming actions. That is broader than the
current BCEHS START decision chart. It is not a direction to perform
every listed action on every patient during a primary triage pass.

28
00:07:57,360 --> 00:08:18,672
BCEHS describes immediate lifesaving interventions in the triage role
as brief, with the current job aid setting a less-than-30-second
target. Use only the action that the posted tool and your authorized
training permit. The triage label must not be delayed by routine care
or by copying a checklist from a different course.

29
00:08:18,672 --> 00:08:33,864
A patient marked Delayed who develops faster breathing, absent radial
pulse or inability to follow commands may enter a different branch.
The correct response is a fresh assessment using the assigned tool,
not a defense of the first tag.

30
00:08:33,864 --> 00:08:47,808
Reassess after a meaningful patient change, new information or a
change in access, staffing or transport capacity. Keep the update
connected to the patient identifier, destination and current
responsible role.

31
00:08:47,808 --> 00:09:01,296
The tag and tracking method may be paper, a numbered triage device or
an approved digital system. Use what the incident plan supplies; do
not invent a parallel list that cannot be reconciled with command.

32
00:09:01,296 --> 00:09:17,712
At every handoff, keep the patient identifier, current priority,
movement, transport unit and receiving destination connected. When
information is uncertain, state who provided it and whether it is
observed, measured or estimated.

33
00:09:17,712 --> 00:09:35,568
The current BCEHS Transport Lead job aid emphasizes ambulance access
and egress, patients ready for transport, destination capacity,
departing-unit records and avoiding ambulance stacking. Those tasks
make patient flow safer and help keep the response capacity visible.

34
00:09:35,568 --> 00:09:49,512
Do not send every patient to the closest emergency department or let
an individual crew self-select a destination. Follow the assigned
transport plan and communicate a new capacity problem before it
creates a queue.

35
00:09:49,512 --> 00:10:10,800
The A&P reading asks you to connect homeostasis and feedback with
triage. A negative-feedback loop uses a response to reduce a
disturbance, while positive feedback can amplify a change. In an
incident, the analogy is that the system observes new information,
takes an authorized action and checks whether the picture improved.

36
00:10:10,800 --> 00:10:25,896
This is a thinking aid, not a physiologic diagnosis and not a
replacement for START. A fast-moving crowd, missing ambulance or
deteriorating patient is a new input; the response is to update the
shared plan and assess again.

37
00:10:25,896 --> 00:10:43,464
MCI triage can create moral distress because available help may not
match every person’s need. The response is not to improvise a private
ranking system. Use the incident’s approved tool, report what it
cannot resolve and request the appropriate clinical or command
decision.

38
00:10:43,464 --> 00:11:00,144
The school’s “greatest good” discussion should not be reduced to a
slogan. Fairness requires clear criteria, consistent use,
communication and re-evaluation; an assigned category does not
describe a person’s value or make uncertainty disappear.

39
00:11:00,144 --> 00:11:16,272
Stress, fatigue, family connections and visual chaos can narrow
attention. A short pause to state the branch, a second-person read-
back and an announced count can reduce avoidable drift without turning
triage into a long examination.

40
00:11:16,272 --> 00:11:32,208
A difficult emotional reaction is not proof of poor character or
incompetence. Use the planned debrief and voluntary support pathways;
do not promise that a single debrief prevents later distress or
require disclosure of personal details.

41
00:11:32,208 --> 00:11:46,152
The AET module discusses limited evidence, human factors and the risk
of both over-triage and under-triage. Avoid teaching unsupported
performance statistics or claiming one tool is universally superior.

42
00:11:46,152 --> 00:12:03,048
A rapid system is useful only when responders know the exact version,
training and escalation path. When conditions are dynamic or the tool
does not fit, use the applicable operational plan and ask for
direction rather than silently editing the decision rule.

43
00:12:03,048 --> 00:12:20,760
The school plan asks for rapid triage tags and an ICS radio report on
Saturday, followed by a more complex Sunday simulation with multiple
vehicles, changing priorities and command rotation. Practise the
published 30-second target while respecting the exact local card.

44
00:12:20,760 --> 00:12:32,592
The course can prepare your reasoning and language, but it cannot
demonstrate your hands-on skill, certify your triage tags or
substitute for the school instructor’s current station instructions.

45
00:12:32,592 --> 00:12:44,952
Teach the adult and pediatric algorithms separately from memory, then
check yourself against the actual current charts. If a threshold or
label differs, correct your notes before the weekend station.

46
00:12:44,952 --> 00:13:00,288
For every patient, connect the tag to a responsible role and a next
step. For every system change, say who needs the update. This is the
difference between memorizing a triage chart and helping an MCI
response work.

47
00:13:00,288 --> 00:13:30,000
Imagine several injured people outside a community venue, with
restricted ambulance access. You have an assigned role, not unlimited
permission to take over. Primary triage creates a temporary priority;
the incident report describes current demand and access. Later, a
patient becomes confused and the intended movement route becomes
slippery. Keep those changes separate, then identify who needs each
update and what action cannot safely wait.

48
00:13:30,000 --> 00:14:01,224
The reading workshop supplies the complete connected explanations,
including research, regulation, transfers, recovery and leadership.
Use them to justify this case rather than memorize eleven unrelated
summaries. The physiology reading explains regulation; the clinical
readings test possible causes; the operational tools organize action;
the professional readings help the team deliver and evaluate it. None
replaces the actual adult or pediatric triage chart.

49
00:14:01,224 --> 00:14:30,264
Negative feedback opposes a disturbance, but compensation does not
prove that the injury or illness has resolved. A confused patient with
a high blood-pressure reading still needs assessment of neurologic
findings, breathing, perfusion and the time course. Hypertensive
emergency concerns acute organ injury, not the number alone. Consider
other threats rather than deciding that every new mental-status change
is hypertensive encephalopathy.

50
00:14:30,264 --> 00:14:59,448
Choose measurements to answer the next clinical question without
postponing immediate care. Explain the technique and relevant
position, timing or equipment differences when reporting a repeat
value. The pain reading adds onset, provocation, quality, region,
severity, timing, treatment and the patient's understanding. A pain
scale supports that history; it neither proves a cause nor replaces
the patient's account or current observations.

51
00:14:59,448 --> 00:15:30,048
The literal CPSC pages concern transfers: assess the patient's
ability, restrictions and tolerance, then choose trained assistance
and appropriate equipment. A slow route is a resource problem, not
permission for an improvised lift. Clear the path, agree who leads the
movement, secure and use equipment as trained, and maintain
communication. Do not transplant a hospital transfer checklist into
ambulance equipment without checking the actual procedure.

52
00:15:30,048 --> 00:16:01,632
The separately named fall section adds environmental and individual
risk reduction. Wet ground, fatigue, cables, confusion and changing
mobility can alter the plan. If a fall occurs, reassess and obtain
appropriate help before continuing; do not automatically lift the
patient straight back up. A controlled lowering technique requires
training and situational judgment. Report a near miss as useful system
information, not as proof that a team member is careless.

53
00:16:01,632 --> 00:16:41,400
The full emergency-care chapter extends beyond triage. In this case,
ask whether confusion could reflect an injury, oxygenation or
perfusion problem, toxic exposure or another medical cause before
assigning a behavioral explanation. In poisoning, support immediate
threats and seek substance-specific guidance. Do not induce vomiting
or assume charcoal is suitable for every ingestion. Do not transfer an
acetaminophen antidote to salicylate poisoning, or use a
benzodiazepine reversal drug automatically for sedation. The reader
names and explains these medication distinctions.

54
00:16:41,400 --> 00:17:15,864
Reassess deterioration even when an earlier tag looked reassuring.
Distal pulses do not exclude compartment syndrome, and a remembered
time window is not a reason to defer escalation. With suspected opioid
toxicity, breathing and ventilation matter more than a pupil shortcut.
For a distressed or assaulted patient, use safety, consent, privacy,
supportive communication and appropriate referral; preserve options
without imposing a universal forensic deadline or an automatic
psychiatric diagnosis.

55
00:17:15,864 --> 00:17:46,920
A delegation label does not remove accountability or give the
recipient a new scope. Check the task, circumstance, person, direction
and supervision. Is the recipient trained, available and equipped now?
State the patient and task, the concern, the limit and when to report
back. The receiver repeats the assignment, and the sender confirms or
corrects it. A message sent is not proof of understanding, completed
care or an accepted handover.

56
00:17:46,920 --> 00:18:20,328
Interprofessional roles contribute different expertise. A transfer
concern may need equipment expertise; a communication barrier may need
an interpreter; ongoing distress may need a supported referral. Keep
the incident reporting structure while welcoming those contributions.
A structured clinical handover names the situation, relevant
background, assessment and requested next step. Confirm who now holds
the task rather than assuming that proximity or an electronic entry
transfers responsibility.

57
00:18:20,328 --> 00:18:56,136
The pharmacology chapter explains United States medication-law history
and the opening ethical principle of autonomy. Learn that context
without treating a United States rule as British Columbia permission.
For an actual task, check legal authority, service direction and
available resources, and whether you are trained and currently
competent. Record what was assessed and done, the response and
unresolved concern. An information system can share data, but it
cannot make an inaccurate entry true or replace a required live
escalation.

58
00:18:56,136 --> 00:19:27,360
The mental-health reading extends care into rehabilitation, recovery,
discharge and community support. Ask what matters to the person, what
help is feasible and who has accepted follow-up. Recovery includes
health, home, purpose and community, not only symptom removal. An
online option may improve access but still requires privacy,
suitability and an escalation plan. Do not promise a service,
appointment or destination that has not been confirmed.

59
00:19:27,360 --> 00:20:02,856
Evidence-based practice combines the best available evidence, clinical
expertise and the patient's situation and preferences. A hierarchy
helps assess a design, but a high label or a small p-value does not
prove that an answer fits this patient. Shared decision-making
explains options and checks understanding. A care bundle groups
evidence-based actions. A bundle score can require every element to be
present. That scoring rule does not authorize ignoring a
contraindication or importing an outdated hospital intervention into
field care.

60
00:20:02,856 --> 00:20:32,928
After the exercise, define the observed problem precisely. Was a route
hazard unreported, a task not understood or a reassessment missed? An
error can occur without harm; an adverse outcome does not by itself
prove an error. Use a small planned improvement, observe the result
and adjust. Distinguish having equipment, following a process and
improving a patient outcome. Those are related measures, not
interchangeable evidence of success.

61
00:20:32,928 --> 00:21:06,744
Leadership is not one personality or a universally best style. An
urgent threat may require clear direction; planning and improvement
benefit from informed participation. The leader explains the goal,
makes safe escalation possible and checks that actual capacity matches
the assignment. A brief sets roles and risks, a huddle adapts to
change, and a debrief examines what actually happened. Psychological
safety supports speaking up. Professional standards and the command
structure still apply.

62
00:21:06,744 --> 00:21:36,312
Finish this case with an observable improvement and a named owner: for
example, confirm the movement route before dispatching the next team
and check whether the change worked. Offer support after a demanding
event while preserving privacy. The leadership chapter also
distinguishes management functions, planned change, mentoring and
credentials. Learn those distinctions in the reader; neither a
leadership certificate nor this course gives a PCP additional clinical
authority.
